streams, system calls and assembly
=================================

Covers io/file.cxx, io/syscall_linux.cxx, io/string.cxx and assembly/.
Everything here knows the OS or the architecture; the rest is portable
C17.  All public functions use C linkage; internal helpers are in
an anonymous namespace.


Streams
-------

A stream is an opaque yupiter_stream.  Internally: descriptor, staging
buffer, counts, eof and error flags, and an output buffering policy.
Three streams exist: yupiter_stdout (fd 1, line-buffered),
yupiter_stderr (fd 2, unbuffered), yupiter_stdin (fd 0).  Input
functions read from any stream.

Stream objects are constant pointers valid for the whole program.

Buffering
---------

Output buffering is selected per stream: unbuffered, line-buffered, or
fully buffered.  Unbuffered streams hand bytes to the kernel
immediately; line-buffered streams flush on a newline; fully buffered
streams flush when the staging buffer fills or on an explicit flush.


Staging
-------

The staging buffer lives in the stream object, so no allocation.
yupiter_stream_write hands staged bytes to the kernel in one write on
flush, full buffer, or a direct large payload.  A payload larger than
the buffer is written directly, with short writes repeated by the
wrapper.  A full buffer stays staged.


System calls
------------

Linux syscall numbers and register conventions per architecture.
yupiter_sys_read, yupiter_sys_write, yupiter_sys_close, yupiter_sys_exit
wrap the trampolines in assembly/yupiter_syscall*.S.  Read, write and
close retry on EINTR.  Raw syscall helpers are used by tests for
short-write scenarios.

Input buffering
---------------

Pushed-back bytes are returned before any bytes from the input buffer.